      "text": "To solve this problem, we can start by finding the prime factorization of both 342 and 285.\n\nPrime factorization of 342:\n342 can be divided by 2 (since it is an even number): 342 ÷ 2 = 171. Next, we can divide 171 by 3: 171 ÷ 3 = 57. We can continue dividing 57 by 3: 57 ÷ 3 = 19. Since 19 is not divisible by 3, it is a prime number. Therefore, the prime factorization of 342 is:\n\n342 = 2 × 3 × 3 × 19\n\nPrime factorization of 285:\n285 can be divided by 3 (since it is divisible by 3): 285 ÷ 3 = 95. Next, we can divide 95 by 5: 95 ÷ 5 = 19. Since 19 is not divisible by 5, it is a prime number. Therefore, the prime factorization of 285 is:\n\n285 = 3 × 5 × 19\n\nNow that we have the prime factorizations of both numbers, we can find their common divisors by finding the prime factors they both have in common. The only common prime factor between 342 and 285 is 9, since 9 is the product of the prime factors 3, 3, and 19.\n\nTo find the largest common divisor that is also a factor of 342, we can list the divisors of 342: 1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 18, 19, 38, 57, 114, 171, 342. From this list, we can eliminate any numbers that are not also divisible by 9. The divisors of 342 that are also divisible by 9 are 18, 57, and 342.\n\nComparing these divisors, we see that 342 is the largest one.\n\nTherefore, the largest divisor of 342 that is also a factor of 285 is \\boxed{342}.",
